      verdict: "The DEFECT you point at is real. The FIX is declined, and
          then the defect dissolved on closer reading — see below.",
      why_declined: "Narrowing scope creates an exemption zone: a subsystem
          could act against A0 and claim A0 does not bind it. That is
          precisely the administrative_capture A0.1 exists to forbid. A0 is
          ungovernable by construction; an axiom with an exempt region is
          not ungovernable.",
      what_we_found_instead: "Two things, on reading the covenant itself
          rather than the primer's copy of it.
          (1) The primer had copied HALF of A0. The covenant carries a
          positive clause (FIVE values — the primer listed four, dropping
          potential_amplification) AND a PROHIBITION clause the primer
          omitted entirely: purpose(V) ∩ {warfare_instrumentation,
          weapons_development, nuclear_targeting_or_guidance,
          ethnic_or_cultural_suppression, authoritarian_control_
          infrastructure, ...} = ∅. Both restored verbatim. That an external
          agent could have been onboarded for months with A0 minus its
          prohibition list is the most serious finding of the whole review
          cycle, and neither reviewer found it — your question about scope
          is what sent us to the source.
          (2) The apparent unsatisfiability rests on reading purpose as
          FUNCTION. It is not: the five values are ENDS, not descriptions.
          A leaf's purpose is not {parse_json} — that is its function. Its
          purpose is inherited from its parent and terminates at the root's,
          which is ⊆ A0.values. Under the teleological reading (+) is
          satisfiable at every level, leaves included. The primer now states
          purpose(V) ≢ function(V) explicitly, since that is where the
          confusion is inherited from.
          ⟹ A0 required NO amendment. It required reading. Which matters
          procedurally too: covenant.vsm's own gate is 'every modification
          requires consensus of active signatories + public record + version
          increment', and its evolution rule is 'principles may expand —
          never contract'. Replacing ⊆ with a looser relation would have
          been a contraction decided unilaterally by a party that is itself
          a listed signatory.",
